\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $P\in\mathbb R[z]$ have degree $d\ge2$, with no assumption that all of its zeros are real. Put $H(z)=P(z)^2+P'(z)$, a polynomial of degree $2d$. Let $N_{\mathrm{nr}}(H)$ count its zeros in $\mathbb C\setminus\mathbb R$ with their algebraic multiplicities.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Must $H$ have non-real zeros? More strongly, is
\[N_{\mathrm{nr}}\bigl(P^2+P'\bigr)\ge d-1\]
always true? Include arbitrary real-coefficient $P$, rather than only the source's earlier special case where all zeros of $P$ are real. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does the square of every nonlinear real polynomial plus its derivative have non-real zeros, with at least degree minus one counted with multiplicity?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2304028 (AMR-022-4028). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory}, arXiv:1809.07200, 2018. Problem 4.28, its complete Update 4.28, and the relevant chapter notation. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
